\subsection{\code{Ggplot2ContourLayerProcessor$generate_selectors()}}{
  Address each drawn curve by its own element

\code{GeomContour} draws every curve in one \code{polylineGrob}, and gridSVG
exports that as one \verb{<polyline>} per piece with an id of the form
\verb{<grob>.1.<n>} -- measured, a two-level field with two peaks gives
\code{GRID.polyline.1.1.1} through \code{.4}. So a curve is addressed by its
position among the pieces, which is what \code{contour_curves()} returns.

The grob carries grid's automatic name rather than one derived from
the geom, so it is found the way a line layer's is -- by position
among the auto-named polylines of the panel. That is the whole reason
\code{polyline_layer_position()} had to learn about this type: a contour
drawn beside a \code{geom_line()} sits in the same candidate list, and a
count that skipped it would give both layers the other's curves.
  \subsection{Usage}{
    \if{html}{\out{<div class="r">}}
